<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/05SQ">Remark <span data-tag="05SQ">7.7.1.3</span>.</a> Let $\mathscr {F}, \mathscr {G}: K \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be as in Definition <a data-tag="05SM" href="/tag/05SM">7.7.1.1</a>, and let $e$ be an edge of the simplicial set $K$. If $\mathscr {F}(e)$ and $\mathscr {G}(e)$ are both isomorphisms in the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, then the diagram (<a data-tag="05SN" href="/tag/05SN">7.89</a>) is automatically a pullback square (see Corollary <a data-tag="048F" href="/tag/048F">7.6.2.39</a>). In particular, to verify that a natural transformation $\gamma : \mathscr {F} \rightarrow \mathscr {G}$ is cartesian, it suffices to check that (<a data-tag="05SN" href="/tag/05SN">7.89</a>) is a pullback square when $e$ is a nondegenerate edge of $K$. </p>
</article>
